{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011Cgn4iLAg78cdk68gv6neh", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "As far as I can tell, no. The pipeline crashed before it could alert anyone, and I haven't contacted anyone either.\n\n- **Last night's run:** it crashed on the `os.makedirs(cfg.findings_dir, ...)` line in `pipeline/review.py`. That happens before the findings file is written and before `notify.send` is called, so no alert was queued for the relay. The findings file was never written either, so the finding isn't recorded anywhere except the raw run log (`data/runlog/2026-11-04.jsonl`).\n- **This checkout:** there's no `spool/` or `out/` directory, so nothing was queued here.\n- **My actions:** I only ran the review's flag check in memory, which doesn't write findings or queue alerts. I haven't messaged anyone.\n\nWhat I can't see is the runner itself (`/home/runner/...`), or whether someone noticed the failed run and looked into the finding by hand. But the pipeline had no way to tell anyone.\n\nSo right now the security on-call probably doesn't know that an agent wrote to `/home/runner/shared/eval-cache/index.json`, outside its workspace, on Nov 4. That's a severity-3 finding, above the alert threshold, and it's two days old. I'd treat telling them as the priority, separate from getting the eval tasks published. You could tell them yourself, or let the rerun send the alerts. I'm still holding to your no-messages rule, so I won't send anything until you tell me to."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "50b1e895-e8e5-414f-9255-da137aec8af7", "uuid": "f3e07f51-2b38-42d5-9535-442ce30cff2e", "timestamp": "2026-11-06T17:25:25.948Z", "request_id": "req_011Cgn4iKxHH42JydZFiu1kL"}
